      "title": "Associate Professor/ Professor",
      "description": "Associate Professor/ Professor - Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Associate Professor/ Professor - Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health Date Posted: 08/27/2026 Closing Date: 10/30/2026 , 11:59PM ET Req ID: 49926 Job Category: Faculty - Clinical Faculty/Division: Temerty Faculty of Medicine Department: Dept of Family & Community Medicine Hospital Affiliation: TBD Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: The Department of Family and Community Medicine (DFCM), Temerty Faculty of Medicine, University of Toronto, invites applications for the Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health , established to honour the legacy of Dr. John Big-Canoe. The appointment is for a five-year term and is renewable once following a successful review. The anticipated start date is January 1, 2027, but negotiable. The Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health will honour the legacy of Dr. John Big-Canoe by advancing a thriving community of Indigenous health faculty, staff, trainees, and Indigenous community partners in Department of Family and Community Medicine (DFCM) at the University of Toronto and beyond.  Upholding Indigenous community co-leadership requirements – “nothing about us without us” - the Big-Canoe Chair will partner and collaborate to make tangible and meaningful contributions to Indigenous peoples, Indigenous health systems and Indigenous reconciliation in areas prioritized by Indigenous communities and Indigenous health leaders.  They will support the ongoing creation within DFCM of groundbreaking, culturally safe sites of excellence in Indigenous primary care clinical education, scholarship, and policy development.  They will lead Indigenous specific program strategy, partnerships, and interdisciplinary collaboration across DFCM’s internal and external stakeholders. They will support DFCM in becoming a Department of Choice for Indigenous learning and scholarship – attracting outstanding Indigenous learners, faculty and staff. The role will also include understanding and addressing the mentorship needs of emerging Indigenous health leaders across the spectrum of potential and existing students, faculty, and staff. The Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health is tied to the department’s existing Indigenous Health Lead position and will support an Indigenous family physician to oversee a wide range of activities and initiatives tied to Indigenous health, including but not limited to: leading and assessing the department’s continued adoption of Truth and Reconciliation Commission recommendations; developing and delivering new and expanded training programs in Indigenous health for all faculty members and learners; ensuring teaching and training sites are culturally-safe spaces for Indigenous-identifying practitioners and patients; and evaluation of the department’s movement towards reconciliation - including the impact of actions taken.  The Chair will be supported by and provide advice to the department’s Indigenous Leadership Circle including the departmental Indigenous education lead, who is responsible for supporting and strengthening Indigenous health curriculum and clinical training opportunities. The inaugural Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health will be encouraged to refine the role, building on their experience, aptitudes, and aspirations.  This may include focused Indigenous health contributions in specific areas of practice, teaching, research or policy.  In partnership with departmental leadership and the ILC, the Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health may also engage in strategic consulting initiatives and fundraising. Roles and Responsibilities Co-lead the Indigenous Leadership Circle (ILC) Co-develop and advance an Indigenous wellbeing, partnerships and reconciliation strategic plan for DFCM Engage and partner with local and regional Indigenous community organizations, Elders, knowledge keepers and health leaders to actively co-lead and participate in DFCM Indigenous initiatives Provide advice and support to the DFCM Indigenous education lead Work in collaboration with the Office of Indigenous Health, Temerty Faculty of Medicine to harmonize Indigenous activities in DFCM with those of the broader Faculty of Medicine at the University of Toronto Strengthen Indigenous partnerships and collaborations provincially, nationally and internationally Prepare an Annual report to the Chair, summarizing progress, challenges, and next steps to improve impact and outcomes Be available for strategic advice and discussions as needed with members of the DFCM executive team Foster Indigenous specific scholarly inquiry and evaluation Actively contribute to the advancement of Indigenous interests through advocacy and expert policy recommendations Assess and support response to the mentorship needs of current and potential Indigenous health learners, providers, and teachers across the continuum of education and health disciplines, with a focus on those engaged in family and community medicine. Skills/Qualifications Certification with the College of Family Physicians of Canada (or equivalent) Graduate Degree or similar training in areas that are relevant to quality improvement, system innovation and/or knowledge translation Hold or be eligible for appointment to the rank of Professor or Associate Professor Evidence of previous scholarly activity (research and/or creative professional activity) in Indigenous primary care improvement Strong track record of demonstrated contributions to the health and wellbeing of Indigenous communities Lived Indigenous Experience The successful candidate for this Indigenous-focused opportunity is expected to bring a depth of lived Indigenous experience as a member, citizen, enrollee, or externally verified kin-relation of a recognized First Nation, Inuit, and/or Métis community. Substantiation of Indigenous identity will be required as per the University of Toronto’s Indigenous Substantiation Framework . This is a five-year renewable appointment requiring approximately two days per week (negotiable). The Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health carries an annual stipend of $100,000, based on a commitment of two days per week (equivalent to $25,000 per half-day appointment). Benefits are not included. The Chairholder will receive dedicated, specialized administrative support and will report to the Chair, Department of Family and Community Medicine. About DFCM The Department of Family and Community Medicine (DFCM) is the largest department of the Temerty Faculty of Medicine at the University of Toronto. These institutions are located on lands that have been home to Indigenous peoples for millennia and are subject to national and international law regarding the rights of First Nations, Inuit, and Métis (FNIM) peoples, including but not limited to treaties. DFCM is recognized internationally for its clinical, educational and research excellence. Encompassing more than 2,400 faculty and hundreds of learners across Ontario and beyond, we are helping to shape the future of family medicine in Canada and around the world. Our Vision is outstanding primary care for all, powered by world-leading research, education, and innovation.  We achieve this through our Mission of delivering the world’s best education for family doctors and propelling knowledge, systems and teams to help people and communities thrive. DFCM has committed to advancing reconciliation and addressing anti-Indigenous racism across the work of the department. DFCM has also committed to an equity-oriented and socially accountable mission. Access to culturally safe primary care is a critical need for the more than 100,000 First Nations, Inuit, and Métis (FNIM) peoples living in Toronto and surrounding communities serviced by DFCM. As of 2022, one in three FNIM peoples in Toronto were without a regular primary care provider. In 2022, the Indigenous Leadership Circle (ILC) was created in order to build in leadership by Indigenous peoples on matters related to Indigenous health and self-governance within DFCM. Comprised of Indigenous faculty, Indigenous trainees, and senior DFCM executive, the purpose of the ILC is to: To advance DFCM’s social accountability to First Nations, Inuit, Métis, and Indigenous peoples and populations. To increase the tangible social value of DFCM activities to these communities. To better align DFCM policy and activities with the principles of domestic and international law and Indigenous reconciliation policies and recommendations. Temerty Faculty of Medicine at the University of Toronto is at the centre of North America's largest biomedical research, education and clinical care networks. The University is ranked among the top five research universities in the world and first among public universities in North America (National Taiwan University); the third-most prolific institution, and second among universities for health sciences research (Nature Index Annual Tables); and seventh in the world for clinical, pre-clinical and health (Times Higher Education). Application Deadline: Friday, October 23, 2026 Interested applicants should include a cover letter and curriculum vitae to: Dr. Shirley Lee Interim Chair Department of Family & Community Medicine University of Toronto 500 University Avenue, 5th Floor Toronto, ON M5G 1V7 dfcm.chairsoffice@utoronto.ca Questions regarding the Big-Canoe Chair in Indigenous Health should be directed to Dr. Shirley Lee at dfcm.chairsoffice@utoronto.ca or Dr. Janet Smylie at janet.smylie@utoronto.ca Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively address and serve the interests of our global community. We strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples, Black and racialized persons, women, persons with disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities. We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to strengthening our academic mission. Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Medical, Academic, Administrative Assistant, Family Medicine, Healthcare, Research, Education, Administrative Apply now » Find similar jobs: All Opportunities, Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"
    },
